"1. J Hypertens. 2026 Oct 1;44(10):1732-1760. doi: 10.1097/HJH.0000000000004411. \nEpub 2026 Jul 27.\n\n2026 ISH guidelines for the management of hypertension in Africa: the \nInternational Society of Hypertension (ISH) African Regional Advisory Group and \nthe Pan African Society of Cardiology (PASCAR).\n\nDoku A(1)(2), Kruger R(3)(4), Asamoah KT(5)(6), Akumiah FK(5), Auala T(7), \nBeheiry H(8), Mutagaywa R(9)(10), Isiguzo G(11), Akintunde AA(12), Dzudie \nA(13)(14)(15), Russell JBW(16), Nguelefack-Mbuyo EP(17), Mamven MH(18), Amaechi \nMU(19), Elfadil SS(20), Barongo A(21), Shirima G(22), Ibrahim TA(23), Ojji \nD(24), Damasceno A(25), Odili A(24), Ogola E(26), Khan N(27), Schutte AE(28), \nJafar TH(29), Williams B(30), Jones ESW(31), Stergiou GS(32).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Department of Medicine and Therapeutics, University of Ghana Medical School, \nAccra, Ghana.\n(2)Department of Public & Occupational Health, University of Amsterdam Medical \nCentre, University of Amsterdam, Amsterdam, The Netherlands.\n(3)Hypertension in Africa Research Team (HART), Faculty of Health Sciences.\n(4)SAMRC Extramural Research Unit for Hypertension and Cardiovascular Disease, \nNorth-West University, Potchefstroom, South Africa.\n(5)National Cardiothoracic Centre, Korle-Bu Teaching Hospital, Accra, Ghana.\n(6)Severance Cardiovascular Hospital, Division of Cardiology, Department of \nMedicine, Yonsei University College of Medicine, Seoul, South Korea.\n(7)Division of Adult Cardiology, Windhoek Central Hospital Complex, Windhoek, \nNamibia.\n(8)Department Physiology, Faculty of Medicine, International University of \nAfrica, Khartoum, Sudan.\n(9)Department of Internal Medicine, School Clinical Medicine, Muhimbili \nUniversity of Health and Allied Sciences.\n(10)Muhimbili Orthopaedic Institute, Dar es Salaam, Tanzania.\n(11)Department of Internal Medicine, Alex Ekwueme Federal University Teaching \nHospital, Abakaliki.\n(12)Department of Medicine, Ladoke Akintola University of Technology, Ogbomoso, \nNigeria.\n(13)Department of Physiology, Faculty of Medicine and Biomedical Sciences, \nUniversity of Yaounde, Yaounde.\n(14)Department of Internal Medicine, Douala General Hospital.\n(15)Clinical Research Education, Networking and Consultancy (CRENC), Douala, \nCameroon.\n(16)Department of Internal Medicine, Faculty of Clinical Sciences & Dentistry, \nCollege of Medicine and Allied Health Sciences, University of Sierra Leone, \nFreetown, Sierra Leone.\n(17)Research Unit of Animal Physiology and Phytopharmacology, Department of \nAnimal Biology, Faculty of Science, University of Dschang, Dschang, Cameroon.\n(18)Department of medicine University of Abuja.\n(19)Department of Human resources Society for Public Health and Social \nDevelopment, Abuja, Nigeria.\n(20)Department of Obstetrics and Gynaecology, Zulekha Hospital Sharjah, Dubai, \nUnited Arab Emirates.\n(21)Department of Paediatric and Child Health, Mwananyama Regional Referral \nHospital.\n(22)Department of Paediatric and Child Health, Muhimbili National Hospital, Dar \nes Salaam, Tanzania.\n(23)Cardiovascular Department, Faculty of Health Sciences, Universite Abdou \nMoumouni, Niamey, Niger.\n(24)Department of Internal Medicine, Faculty of Clinical Sciences, College of \nHealth Sciences, University of Abuja, Abuja, Nigeria.\n(25)Department of Internal Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, Eduardo Mondlane \nUniversity, Maputo, Mozambique.\n(26)Department of Clinical Medicine and Therapeutics, University of Nairobi. \nNairobi, Kenya.\n(27)Division of General Internal Medicine, Center for Advancing Health Outcomes, \nUniversity of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC, Canada.\n(28)School of Population Health, Faculty of Medicine and Health, University of \nNew South Wales; The George Institute for Global Health, Sydney, Australia.\n(29)Programme in Health Services Research & Population Health, Duke-NUS Medical \nSchool, Singapore.\n(30)Institute of Cardiovascular Sciences, University College London (UCL); \nNational Institute for Health Research UCL Hospitals Biomedical Research Centre, \nLondon, UK.\n(31)Groote Schuur Hospital, Kidney and Hypertension Research Unit, University of \nCape Town, Cape Town, South Africa.\n(32)Hypertension Center STRIDE-7, National and Kapodistrian University of \nAthens, School of Medicine, Third Department of Medicine, Sotiria Hospital, \nAthens, Greece.\n\nHypertension in sub-Saharan Africa is escalating, with many cases undiagnosed \nand poorly controlled, even among children, despite its significant contribution \nto cardiovascular disease. Poor blood pressure control is largely due to limited \nnational guidelines, underfunded programs, and a shortage of trained health \nprofessionals. Simplified protocols for non-physician health workers, who \ndeliver most of the primary care, are essential. Cultural myths and \nmisconceptions further delay care, underscoring the need for community-based \neducation. Early and opportunistic screening, including in schools, markets, and \nplaces of worship, is vital across the life course. Sustainable progress depends \non tailored strategies, investment in equipment and training, and leveraging \nexisting infrastructure to improve hypertension detection and management. \nAfrica-centred hypertension guidelines are now a priority because international \nmodels fail to address the continent's unique clinical, cultural and resource \nrealities. The African Regional Advisory Group of the International Society of \nHypertension worked with the Pan African Society of Cardiology to develop \nsimplified protocols for primary care, particularly for non-physician health \ncare workers, by considering the special situation and condition for managing \nthe vast majority of cases within hypertension in Africa.\n\nCopyright \u00a9 2026 Wolters Kluwer Health, LLC. All rights reserved.\n\nDOI: 10.1097/HJH.0000000000004411\nPMID: 42554264 [Indexed for MEDLINE]"